From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Velez Garcia <velezgarciaemmanuel25@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 23:00:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Correct ghost blocks left behind by mining

A 1.8 client removes a block the moment it believes it broke it, without
waiting to be told. Usually the server agrees. On a prison server it
often does not: the mine regenerates under the player mid-swing, a plugin
puts the block back, or the acknowledgement is lost - and the client is
left showing air where the server has stone, so the player swings at
nothing until something else forces a chunk update.

Blocks a player digs are now remembered and the server's actual view of
them re-sent shortly afterwards.

The delay is the whole design. Correcting immediately is worse than the
bug, because the packet lands while the client is still legitimately
predicting the break and the block visibly flickers back before breaking
again. A disagreement only means something once a round trip has passed,
so the delay scales with ping: minDelayTicks plus ticksPerHundredMsPing
per 100 ms, capped at maxDelayTicks.

  mining.ghostBlockFix.enabled                on by default
  mining.ghostBlockFix.minDelayTicks          3
  mining.ghostBlockFix.ticksPerHundredMsPing  2
  mining.ghostBlockFix.maxDelayTicks          20
  mining.ghostBlockFix.maxBlocksPerResync     32

Batching falls out of it: a player mining faster than their delay has
several positions corrected in one pass rather than one packet per block.
Past the cap the oldest entries are dropped rather than the newest, since
ghosts pile up at the head of a tunnel and not behind it.

The per-player state is allocated on the first dig, so players who never
break a block never pay for it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

